{"archetypes":[{"name":"ship-state","focus_area":"risk-integration","weight":1,"rationale":"The diff flips the default ship driver and changes cross-file state/finalize/restore contracts where regressions can strand /implement runs.","prompt_body":"Investigate the Python-default Step 8+ ship driver path across python/ship.py, skills/implement/SKILL.md, stall recovery, conflict resolution, and related tests. Focus on state-file merging, terminal finalize writes, restore-finalize gating, exit-code JSON routing, transient and needs-user re-entry behavior, and bash opt-in compatibility. Look for any path that still routes default Python continuation through the bash exit matrix or stale ship-pr-state.sh parsing. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"bash32","focus_area":"code-quality","weight":1,"rationale":"Many shell changes add awk/sed/grep parsing and harness logic that must remain portable and quote-safe in larch shell conventions.","prompt_body":"Review shell scripts and tests for Bash 3.2 compatibility, safe quoting, pipefail behavior, temp-file assumptions, and robust parsing of marker or prompt boundaries. Pay special attention to scripts under scripts/ and skills/design/scripts/ that use awk windows, grep pins, XML-like tags, or injected file paths. Check whether new helpers preserve quiet-mode and executable-mode conventions where applicable. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"prompt-safety","focus_area":"security","weight":1,"rationale":"The diff introduces untrusted scope-anchor and voter prompt rendering paths that could accidentally execute or leak user-controlled content.","prompt_body":"Examine prompt rendering and subprocess dispatch changes that inline untrusted plan, ballot, diff, or scope-anchor content. Focus on escaping, redaction, tag-boundary handling, instruction-injection resistance, and whether wrappers clearly separate evidence from commands. Include render-voter-prompt, render-plan-review-prompt, scope-anchor renderers, and dynamic archetype scouting paths. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"plan-voting","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan-review scope-reduction marker and anchored voter logic spans dispatch, rendering, tallying, aggregation, and tests.","prompt_body":"Trace the new plan-review scope-anchor and scope-reduction marker behavior end to end. Verify marker detection semantics, dispatch arguments, prompt text, voter tally interpretation, aggregation, and fallback behavior when anchor files are missing or irrelevant. Look for mismatches where a marker is counted, ignored, promoted, or stripped differently across the pipeline. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"contract-drift","focus_area":"architecture","weight":1,"rationale":"The implementation updates runtime prompts, docs, tests, and generated-style contract files where inconsistent prose can become runtime behavior.","prompt_body":"Review whether user-facing docs, skill prompts, markdown companion files, tests, and runtime scripts describe the same contracts. Focus on python-default versus bash-opt-in wording, Step 18 restore behavior, stall classification layers, plan-review scope-anchor semantics, and any generated or companion documentation that may be stale. Treat prompt prose as executable workflow specification when it controls larch behavior. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
